Matches stable Speculative Markets 1.3.

[b]Growth[/b]
[list]
[*]Good credit now builds. Years of well-run borrowing earn [b]Sound Credit[/b], which makes construction faster and grows toward 1.67% for each month of leverage. Panics and Bad Debts wear it away, faster the deeper the leverage.
[*]Booms build more. An industry builds 15% faster with every good it makes Eager and 30% with every good Feverish (was 10% and 20%). A good counts by its share of its industry's trade, and more goods now belong to an industry.
[*]Crises cost construction. A panic cuts construction efficiency by 30% while it lasts, and each point of Bad Debts by 5%.
[*]The Construction effect on the Speculation tab shows each industry's bonus. Hover over one to see the goods behind it, with their icons and Investor Interest.
[*]New game rule, [b]Market Impact[/b], scales how much leverage, panics and Bad Debts affect your economy. Flavor is about two fifths of Standard, and Strong is about three fifths more.
[/list]

[b]Booms, banks and panics[/b]
[list]
[*]Booms land softer. A boom whose price stays at or above where it began can only end in a Correction. A Bust needs the price to fall below where the boom began, and a Collapse needs it to fall well below. Building to meet real demand is no longer punished as a Collapse.
[*]Bank rescues no longer make banks laxer. A panic still sobers rescued banks, by half as much as banks left to fail. Bad Debts now make banks stricter each month, twice as fast under Tight credit. Before, they held Lending Standards still.
[*]Lending Standards show their number and this month's change, such as Lax 78.8 (+0.18).
[*]The Market Confidence card says whether anything needs you, and its tooltip what you can do about it.
[/list]

[b]Contests[/b]
[list]
[*]Contests now read like Liberty Desire. Each has a number from 0 (theirs) to 100 (ours), starts at 50 and shows this month's change. Hover over the bar for what each result gives, everything that moves the contest and where it will settle.
[*]Moves play out over months. Bear Raid and Shore Up cost a share of their price each month, and each push arrives a month at a time. Lure Capital's debt also arrives a month at a time, so it no longer sets off a panic the moment it is taken.
[*]If the treasury cannot pay a month of a Bear Raid or Shore Up, the move ends. An unpaid Bear Raid gives back the rest of its hit on the target.
[*]A Lure ends if a panic begins, and its push is lost. Its tooltip warns when its debt would lift the panic line above your Market Confidence.
[*]A short notice tells you when one of your campaigns ends early.
[*]Shore Up again shows the right dates when it is used more than once on the same competitor.
[*]The action tooltips are much shorter.
[/list]

[b]Interface[/b]
[list]
[*]The Exchange tab's world list shows every Provincial market, best first. Each tier can be folded away.
[*]The Exchange Score and Trust headings show this month's change.
[*]Pinned goods in the outliner show the good's Investor Interest and the ending its boom would reach.
[*]Trust gains and losses from Blue Chips are shown in points.
[*]A good's throughput from Investor Interest, Encourage and Restrain, and a recent company crash now sits on its buildings as one modifier. The amounts are the same.
[/list]

[b]Other balance[/b]
[list]
[*]High leverage lifts the confidence a panic starts at by up to 55 points (was 50).
[*]Bad Debts fade by 0.35 points a month (was 0.3).
[*]Buying up the banks' bad loans clears 25% of the remaining Bad Debts (was 30%).
[*]A country's income counts as at least 0.9% of its GDP a month when working out leverage (was 1%).
[*]Lure Capital's debt arrives over 12 months, half a month of the investment pool each month (was all 6 months at once).
[/list]

Saves from 1.2 carry on. Contest moves already under way are paid in full and run as they were.